    "Reward hacking. Particularly insidious in RLHF [17, 18], is the reward hacking issue [19, 20, 21, 22] (a.k.a. reward overoptimization), arising from reward misspecification [23, 24] between the proxy $\\mathfrak{m}(R)$ and actual human preferences. While optimizing for the RM initially provides improvements, in later stages the policy ($R$) and actual human preference may diverge, causing the model to learn to exploit loopholes in the RM and achieve high rewards without truly fulfilling the intended objectives, as illustrated in Figure 1(b). This reward hacking phenomenon poses numerous issues. First, it degrades system performance [25]. Second, it complicates checkpoint selection due to the unreliability of the proxy RM, echoing Goodhart’s Law [27]: 'When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.' Third, it can endanger human safety [28, 29] or amplify social biases, reflecting real-world bias and decision-making problems [30, 31]. Lastly, it is also critical, as misalignment [32, 33] due to reward hacking can escalate into risky scenarios [$19, 34, 35]$, particularly when the reward function drifts in everyday life and critical situations. Such concerns underscore the need to mitigate reward hacking to ensure the beneficial and safe deployment of LLMs.",
    "WARM: On the Benefits of Weight Averaged Reward Models",
    "Aligning large language models (LLMs) with human preferences through reinforcement learning (RLHF) can lead to reward hacking, where LLMs exploit failures in the reward models (RMs) to achieve seemingly high rewards without meeting the underlying objectives. We identify two primary challenges when designing RMs to mitigate reward hacking: distribution shifts during the $\\mathfrak{m}$-training (RLHF) process and inconsistencies in human preferences. As a solution, we propose Weight Averaged Reward Models (WARM): first, distilling multiple RMs, then averaging them in the weight space. By aggregating weights, WARM shows that the reward models remain linearly connected when sharing the same pre-training tasks, using best-of-$\\mathfrak{m}$ and RL methods, which has a $97.4\%$ improvement over a single RM. Our experiments on summarization tasks, using example-a, $\\mathfrak{m}$ and RL fine-tuned with WARM, show that WARM improves the overall quality and alignment of LLM predictions compared to a single RM."
  ]
}
